#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157044 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "35283781-7e25-5144-9bdd-77921cf5ea26" :question/math-academy-id "e-16703" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2x-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x-2{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2+4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{2(0.5)-2{e}^{-2(0.5)}}{2+4{e}^{-2(0.5)}} \\\\  & ≈0.424,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157045 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "1bf35491-4dda-5bff-9a9e-c3e91f9d5edc" :question/math-academy-id "e-16704" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-12x+9, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}-12x+9)^{′}=6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}-12{x}_{n}+9}{6{x}_{n}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}-12{x}_{0}+9}{6{x}_{0}-12} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}-12(0)+9}{6(0)-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}+9}{6{x}_{1}-12} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{3(0.75)^{2}-12(0.75)+9}{6(0.75)-12} \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and $x_2 = 0.975.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157046 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/id #uuid "67b4f920-532d-56dc-8b6b-c76d5115959d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16705" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3x+1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-3)^{′}=12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{3}-(4{x}_{n}^{3}-3)}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}_{n}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{8{x}_{0}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1)^{3}+3}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.916\\,666….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{8{x}_{1}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.916\\,666…)^{3}+3}{12(0.916\\,666…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,631…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{8{x}_{2}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.908\\,631…)^{3}+3}{12(0.908\\,631…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,560….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx0.91$ and $x_3 \\approx 0.91$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x \\approx 0.91,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.886" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.886" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512182} :answer-field/id #uuid "784f0dbb-d926-5461-9f9f-98f165e27f6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c86499a3-b11b-54e3-8a70-04bd4551cb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-256464" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+2x-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}+2x-3)^{′}=6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}-3}{6{x}_{n}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}-3}{6{x}_{0}+2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3}{6(0)+2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-3}{6{x}_{1}+2} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{3(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)-3}{6(1.5)+2} \\\\  & ≈0.886,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.309" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.309" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512644} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f567585-3cf1-5a7c-a653-205c6d1fd118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-256783" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5)^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-4x+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-4x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4(3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-4{x}_{n}+1}{4(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-4{x}_{0}+1}{4(3{x}_{0}^{2}-1)} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{4(1.8)^{3}-4(1.8)+1}{4(3(1.8)^{2}-1)} \\\\  & ≈1.309,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512235} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a5ed95-b9e5-5fc7-bee1-be473b9bf6cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" :question/math-academy-id "q-256788" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x}$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-2x+2-(-{x}^{2}+2x+1)){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}{({x}_{n}^{2}-4{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(-{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}}{({x}_{0}^{2}-4{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(-(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}}{((1.5)^{2}-4(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}} \\\\  & ≈2.136,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512467} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ccc8ea8-3dd2-55a3-84bd-b9f4206be0d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607" :question/math-academy-id "q-256797" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =((1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(-1-2x(1-x)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =((2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(4x-2-2x(2{x}^{2}-2x-1)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(2{x}_{n}^{2}-2{x}_{n}-1){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}-2){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(2{x}_{0}^{2}-2{x}_{0}-1){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{0}^{3}+4{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}-2){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(2(1.5)^{2}-2(1.5)-1){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}}{(-4(1.5)^{3}+4(1.5)^{2}+6(1.5)-2){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512147} :answer-field/id #uuid "86bdc7aa-e58d-58a2-934c-5e0eb671151b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-256804" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1}$  in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{0}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{0}+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{0.8+2}-\\frac{1}{(0.8+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{(0.8+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(0.8+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈0.575,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da01b8a7-bbe2-57aa-931d-3fc1f54e4b9e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512488} :answer-field/id #uuid "63671e40-590f-536b-8dfb-757855c8f75f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbd4e823-0309-5688-94b6-82a55c915b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-256810" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}$  in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=-1.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3})^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+3{x}_{n}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-4{x}_{n}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+3{x}_{0}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-4{x}_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})+3(-1)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})-4(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2})+6(-1)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-3}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-6} \\\\  & ≈-0.768,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.306" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.306" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512497} :answer-field/id #uuid "db36be24-0165-5b4e-82e0-fd0653f9f4e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" :question/math-academy-id "q-256818" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3)^{′}=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2)^{′}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3} \\\\  & =1.367\\,897…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =(1.367\\,897…)-\\frac{2(1.367\\,897…)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+(1.367\\,897…)-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+3} \\\\  & ≈1.306,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512671} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c09538-67a9-5afd-bd61-be1b48c03142" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-256825" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-24{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{5{x}_{0}^{4}-12{x}_{0}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-24{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5(2)^{4}-12(2)^{2}+1}{20(2)^{3}-24(2)} \\\\  & =1.705\\,357…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{5{x}_{1}^{4}-12{x}_{1}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-24{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.705\\,357…)-\\frac{5(1.705\\,357…)^{4}-12(1.705\\,357…)^{2}+1}{20(1.705\\,357…)^{3}-24(1.705\\,357…)} \\\\  & ≈1.561,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.693" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.693" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512599} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af7dcdc-a31a-5223-ae74-a699e51c827c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" :question/math-academy-id "q-256826" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2x-1$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2x-1)^{′}={e}^{x}-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{0.5}-2}{{e}^{0.5}} \\\\  & =0.713\\,061…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =(0.713\\,061…)-\\frac{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}-2}{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}} \\\\  & ≈0.693,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.435" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.435" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512667} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bab8a8-2a72-5135-8859-4f7ad2ce0f46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512690} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b9b8c1-e860-5fe5-b064-419ade05631e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-256860" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x$ in the interval $[0.2,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.2.$ Round your answers to three decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x)^{′}=\\frac{2}{x}-4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{2}{x}-4)}^{′}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}}-4}{-\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+{x}_{n}(1-2{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}(1-{x}_{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}(1-{x}_{0}) \\\\  & =2(0.2)(1-0.2) \\\\  & =0.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}(1-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & =2(0.32)(1-0.32) \\\\  & =0.4352\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}=0.32$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.435,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb7d3c3-f99e-5007-8ec4-1cbb177d4b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512166} {:answer/id #uuid "5c10bba8-b37f-5bb1-804a-6d8a5cbebc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512312} {:answer/id #uuid "5d68c59f-4f44-585f-ada6-6e1af634b4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512317} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512365} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d96544-e96b-56d9-bceb-3eaf6a5f5840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512365} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcfedb6-87ce-541d-becd-78995b9b5d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367472" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x)^{′}=5{x}^{4}+6x-1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}+6x-1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}+6)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1)}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.5)^{4}+1}{20(0.5)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.227\\,941….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.227\\,941…)^{4}+1}{20(0.227\\,941…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,829…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{2}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.166\\,829…)^{4}+1}{20(0.166\\,829…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,033….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.17$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.17$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.17,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28160efb-5c1f-5bdd-ab7b-b9c2c3928a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512188} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a7928-3480-546a-a1db-608d12f49372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512260} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512325} {:answer/id #uuid "8e309a29-9c1e-5920-a47f-96c7863b8574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512432} {:answer/id #uuid "f201bc27-4c58-5510-a349-d7278294decc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.07" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512325} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb7f079-1f2a-5896-b4e4-a2128ff0ff05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-367477" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.2$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4)^{′}={e}^{x}-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-12{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-12{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{{e}^{1.2}-6(1.2)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.2}-12(1.2)} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-12{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875…-\\frac{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-6(1.080\\,875…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-12(1.080\\,875…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6{x}_{2}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-12{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…-\\frac{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-6(1.074\\,640…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-12(1.074\\,640…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,623….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.07$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.07$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512152} {:answer/id #uuid "5195019a-0822-52d4-9694-c18192ce17ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512279} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf6a465-36cf-5de1-9103-6e46c194df81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512549} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f14a15-a50b-506e-9ef6-fbe0f2fd1732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512568} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e7e852-c97b-521c-b40c-6c022ad8b9c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512152} :answer-field/id #uuid "c871c6f1-367b-5fb5-b189-b4789899d2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" :question/math-academy-id "q-367494" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,0.5],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2})^{′}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x=2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3x), \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-6=-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n})}{-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})-3{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =0.5+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)-3\\cdot 0.5}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})-3{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)-3\\cdot 0.272\\,999…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…., \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{2})-3{x}_{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{2})+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)-3\\cdot 0.283\\,140…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,068….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.28$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.28$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.28,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c3f308-2cf6-56a0-b5d5-965d575f5ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512137} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512201} {:answer/id #uuid "7a331c84-ec7a-5cf3-8a41-1b4c4fc250b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512388} {:answer/id #uuid "b59f4ce9-57f0-5b4c-ab7f-71027d3c81f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512530} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4a2150-a8b4-5a78-9311-6c6806e53953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512201} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ee9a01-2e0f-5959-b627-c127878594e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-367495" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.65$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12x)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}-12)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n})}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.65)^{4}}{20(1.65)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.428\\,269…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.428\\,269…)^{4}}{20(1.428\\,269…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.349\\,003…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}}{20{x}_{2}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.349\\,003…)^{4}}{20(1.349\\,003…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.339\\,016…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{15{x}_{3}^{4}}{20{x}_{3}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.339\\,016…)^{4}}{20(1.339\\,016…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.338\\,865….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.34$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.34$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.34,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512190} {:answer/id #uuid "549fb8a0-48d5-5f7f-b520-c39d00f8ecef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512290} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3c7483-fb38-539f-8efc-25641745f0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512399} {:answer/id #uuid "90f997ea-c7bb-5fd8-bca1-7ccbea8af2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512443} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d0f5c4-36ba-5792-b2b1-be19c6791c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512190} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e9f9e09-53f9-57a2-9d1e-832e556456f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-389801" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.8$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2})^{′}=6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x)^{′}=30{x}^{4}-40{x}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2)-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n})}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}-30{x}_{n}^{4}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{5}-15{x}_{n}^{4}}{15{x}_{n}^{4}-20{x}_{n}^{3}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{12{x}_{0}^{5}-15{x}_{0}^{4}}{15{x}_{0}^{4}-20{x}_{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.8)^{5}-15(1.8)^{4}}{15(1.8)^{4}-20(1.8)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.656\\,564….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{12{x}_{1}^{5}-15{x}_{1}^{4}}{15{x}_{1}^{4}-20{x}_{1}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.656\\,564…)^{5}-15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}}{15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}-20(1.656\\,564…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.594\\,573…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{12{x}_{2}^{5}-15{x}_{2}^{4}}{15{x}_{2}^{4}-20{x}_{2}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.594\\,573…)^{5}-15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}}{15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}-20(1.594\\,573…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,950…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{12{x}_{3}^{5}-15{x}_{3}^{4}}{15{x}_{3}^{4}-20{x}_{3}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.582\\,950…)^{5}-15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}}{15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}-20(1.582\\,950…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,567….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.58$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.58$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.58,$ rounded to two decimal places."}]}
